Here the nodes are first stored in a Go slice. That gives direct access to the node before the insertion point and keeps the link update easy to see. The slice stores pointers, not copies of the nodes, so the original chain is still changed in place.

For `2 -> 5 -> 8` and `K = 3`, the slice points to nodes 2, 5, and 8. The new node is linked after the second pointer, producing `2 -> 5 -> X -> 8`.

The extra slice space is the trade-off for simpler position handling.

```go
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
type ListNode struct {
    Data int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func insertAtKthPosition(head *ListNode, X int, K int) *ListNode {
    newNode := &ListNode{Data: X}

    if K <= 1 || head == nil {
        newNode.Next = head
        return newNode
    }

    nodes := make([]*ListNode, 0)
    current := head
    for current != nil {
        nodes = append(nodes, current)
        current = current.Next
    }

    previousIndex := K - 2
    if previousIndex >= len(nodes) {
        previousIndex = len(nodes) - 1
    }

    previous := nodes[previousIndex]
    newNode.Next = previous.Next
    previous.Next = newNode
    return head
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the slice of node pointers.

The recursive helper counts the current position while moving through the list. When it reaches the node just before `K`, it links the new node there. A position of 1 is handled before recursion so an empty list and head insertion are safe.

Dry run for `10 -> 20 -> 30`, `X = 15`, `K = 2`:

- The first call is at position 1, so it moves to node 20.
- Node 20 is at position 2, so the new node is linked before it.
- The result is `10 -> 15 -> 20 -> 30`.

This uses call-stack space, unlike an iterative version.

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode insertAtKthPosition(ListNode head, int X, int K) {
        ListNode newNode = new ListNode(X);

        if (K <= 1 || head == null) {
            newNode.next = head;
            return newNode;
        }

        insertAfterPrevious(head, newNode, K, 1);
        return head;
    }

    private void insertAfterPrevious(ListNode current, ListNode newNode, int K, int position) {
        if (position == K - 1 || current.next == null) {
            newNode.next = current.next;
            current.next = newNode;
            return;
        }

        insertAfterPrevious(current.next, newNode, K, position + 1);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Space complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case because of recursion.

I used a small dummy node before the list. This makes the normal insertion and the head insertion follow almost the same pointer logic. For `K <= 1`, the new node becomes the head. For larger positions, the pointer stops just before the requested position.

If `K` is past the current length, this code places the node at the end. The input list is changed in place.

```cpp
class Solution {
    public:
        ListNode* insertAtKthPosition(ListNode* &head, int X, int K) {
            ListNode* newNode = new ListNode(X);

            if (K <= 1) {
                newNode->next = head;
                head = newNode;
                return head;
            }

            ListNode dummy(0, head);
            ListNode* previous = &dummy;

            for (int position = 1; position < K && previous->next != nullptr; position++) {
                previous = previous->next;
            }

            newNode->next = previous->next;
            previous->next = newNode;
            head = dummy.next;
            return head;
        }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case. Space complexity: `O(1)` besides the new node.

This version first counts the nodes, then chooses the exact insertion point. It also makes positions larger than the list length behave as an append by limiting the target position to `length + 1`.

Example with `4 -> 7 -> 9`, `X = 6`, and `K = 2`: the length is 3, the target stays 2, and the pointer moves to node 4 before linking node 6.

The list is modified directly, and the empty-list case is covered by the first condition.

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode insertAtKthPosition(ListNode head, int X, int K) {
        ListNode newNode = new ListNode(X);

        if (K <= 1 || head == null) {
            newNode.next = head;
            return newNode;
        }

        int length = 0;
        ListNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            length++;
            current = current.next;
        }

        int target = Math.min(K, length + 1);
        current = head;

        for (int position = 1; position < target - 1; position++) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        newNode.next = current.next;
        current.next = newNode;
        return head;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since the list can be visited twice. Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the new node.

